flags: -O

--- OUT --->
3
--- IR --->
// the window's length and data pointer are loaded **once, in the entry block** - the cursor holds its
// bound by value, so nothing in the loop can have written them and LICM may hoist both. that is what
// gives the loop a computable trip count, and with it unrolling and vectorisation
CHECK: define i32 @_totalZZMRBMLC5arrayIMLPiE
CHECK: entry:
CHECK: load i64
CHECK: load ptr

// **the loop vectorises, and that is the assertion.** it is a strictly stronger statement than "the body
// holds no calls": a loop LoopVectorize widened is one it could prove has no calls, no aliasing hazard and
// a computable trip count - so `iterate`, `advance` and `current` are inlined away and the cursor struct is
// SROA'd to a register, or none of this would be here.
//
// worth knowing why it is spelled this way. it used to read `CHECK: body:` / `CHECK-NOT: call`, and it kept
// passing after vectorisation was switched on - but for the wrong reason: the directives are plain
// substrings, so `body:` matched `vector.body:` and the test silently moved from describing the scalar loop
// to describing the vector one. Asking for the vector body by name is what makes that deliberate
CHECK: vector.body:
CHECK-NOT: call
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: load <4 x i32>
CHECK: add <4 x i32>

// and the scalar tail - the elements past the last full vector - is still the indexed load and the loop's
// own compare, with nothing else in it. the `llvm.vector.reduce.add` that folds the accumulators sits in
// `middle.block` between the two, outside either loop, which is why neither CHECK-NOT region covers it
CHECK: getelementptr i32
CHECK-NOT: call
CHECK-NOT: alloca
CHECK: br i1
